{"id": "63a4ef4d-1fb8-414c-9568-f119eaacc285", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "Good news: Q Business is explicitly designed to work with an IAM Identity Center instance in a different region from the application — cross-region IdC is a supported, common topology (it's actually the norm, since most orgs have one org-level IdC instance). So it's unlikely to be the direct cause on its own, but there are specific cross-region details (like the IdC instance ARN being correctly referenced, and the identity-aware session/auth flow) that can break chat if misconfigured.

Let me check the actual Q Business application config and its IAM Identity Center attachment to see what's really going on.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_mZBC8zLMGrs4KxO6aa1W8P", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"prompt": "{
  "task": "Diagnose why users cannot chat with an Amazon Q Business application. The customer's hypothesis is that the Q Business application lives in us-east-1 while their IAM Identity Center instance is an organization-level instance in us-west-2, and they suspect this cross-region setup is the cause.",
  "context": "Determine whether cross-region IAM Identity Center is actually a valid root cause, or a red herring, by inspecting the real configuration. Cross-region attachment between Q Business and an org-level IAM Identity Center instance is a normal, supported AWS pattern (IdC instances are commonly centralized in one region while consuming apps live elsewhere) — so don't assume region mismatch alone is the bug. Instead verify the actual wiring: does the Q Business application's IdentityCenterInstanceArn correctly reference the us-west-2 org instance, is the Q Business application in a healthy/ACTIVE state, is there a valid IdentityCenterApplicationArn (the IdC-managed application AWS creates for Q Business), and are users/groups actually assigned to that IdC application. Also check for the more common real causes of 'users cannot chat' failures: missing user/group subscription or assignment in Identity Center, Q Business application not ACTIVE, missing or misconfigured service role / trust policy for the Q Business app, retriever/index issues, or plugin/role permission denials. Use the aiml-access-diagnostics skill methodology (trace the authorization chain: caller identity, trust policy, role permissions, resource policies) if it helps structure the check, since this is an AI/ML access-shaped issue. List Q Business applications in us-east-1 for this account to find the app ID if not already known, then describe it and its IdC attachment.",
  "resources": ["Q Business application (region us-east-1)", "IAM Identity Center org instance (region us-west-2)"],
  "timeframe": "current state as of now",
  "criteria": "Confirm: (1) the Q Business app's identityCenterInstanceArn matches the real org IdC instance, (2) application status is ACTIVE, (3) an IdC-managed application ARN exists for Q Business, (4) whether users/groups are assigned, (5) any other access-shaped misconfiguration (role trust policy, missing permissions) that would block chat. Report the actual root cause if found, and confirm/refute the cross-region hypothesis explicitly."
}", "deep_research": false}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Diagnose Q Business chat failures tied to cross-region IAM Identity Center setup"}], "created_at": null}

